Part 1 - Fraud Detection


What is the business problem?

A Payment or Bank institution is interested in stopping fraudulent activity prior to the funds being removed from a customer account. Currently, many cases of fraud are only detected after the customer has reported fraud. This results in the funds often being already depleted. The institution is interested in a system that will examine each transaction as it occurs and attempt to identify any fraudulent transactions. Essentially this would be like having a security person at every transaction rather than only looking after the fact that fraud has occurred.

Why is this problem important to the organization?

Fraud results in actual financial loss to the company, as money may have to be given back to a consumer for transactions that were not in fact made by that consumer. It can also cause a loss of consumer confidence. Consumers will begin to move to another card or financial institution if they believe their funds are at risk. Fraud detection at an early stage allows a financial institution to avoid penalties and litigation. Financial institutions are obligated to detect fraudulent transactions. In addition, it is far less expensive to prevent fraud in real time rather than to handle fraud after the fact. Response to and refunding of fraudulent transactions require the use of time and resources from employees.


Who is affected by this problem?

Customers
Finance department
Fraud/risk management team
Customer support team
Managers/leadership
Legal/compliance department



Part 2 — Identify the Data — 10 Points

What data would you need?	Transaction amount, location of purchase, time of day, type of merchant, device used, how often this pattern is normal for that customer

Where could the data come from?	Payment processing systems, banking app logs, merchant records, customer account history, device/login data

How much historical data might be useful?	At least 1–2 years of past transactions, including confirmed fraud cases and normal ones

What would be the most important variables?	Unusual transaction amount, unfamiliar location, sudden spending spike, new device, odd time of day'

What could be missing or inaccurate?	Incomplete merchant info, mislabeled fraud cases, missing location data, delayed transaction records

Part 3 — Identify Features and Target — 10 Points

Features (the clues we give the model):

Transaction amount
Location of the purchase
Time of day/day of week
Merchant category (e.g., gas station, online store)
Device or IP address used
How different this transaction is from the customer's normal spending habits

Target (what we want the model to guess):

Is this transaction fraudulent? Yes or No


Would the problem involve classification or prediction?
Choose:

I chose supervised learning due to the existance of previously known fraud or not fraud transactions. This is similar to a teacher marking homework. A correct answer is provided (fraud = yes/no) for each example and the pattern is learned. Because there is previously known information we are able to train the model on known information rather than pure guessing. If there was no information about previously known fraud then unsupervised learning would need to be used. This would look for anomalies or unusual behaviour but would not provide definite fraud information.

Classification as it labels every transaction as either fraud or not fraud. Like letters being put into two different boxes. Also a prediction because the system makes an on the spot decision (before the decision has actually been made whether or not it is fraud) by using predicted probabilities. Similar to a security scanner at an airport identifying a bag as suspicious. This is both classification and prediction.

Part 5 — Data Quality and Business Impact — 10 Points

Identify three possible data-quality problems.
Examples:

Three possible data-quality problems:

Missing information — some transactions might be missing location or device data
Mislabeled data — a fraud case might be incorrectly marked as "normal" (or vice versa) in the training data
Inconsistent formats — different systems recording currency, timestamps, or merchant names differently

Poor or incorrect data can result in the model learning incorrect information (e.g., by using error prone data for training). As a result, legitimate transactions may be blocked to the detriment of the customer (and perception that their card is not working) or fraudulent transactions may go undetected because the model was not adequately trained to identify fraud. Biased or poor data may also lead to disparate treatment of certain customer segments. In short: **bad data in → bad predictions out → bad business decisions.**

Part 6 — Complete AI Solution Summary

Business Problem:	Detect fraudulent transactions in real time, before money is lost.
Data Needed:	Transaction amount, location, time, merchant type, device info, and the customer's normal spending habits.
AI/ML Approach:	A model that predicts and classifies each transaction as fraud or not fraud.
Type of Learning:	Supervised learning — because the company has past transactions already labeled as fraud or not fraud.
Model Output:	A fraud risk label for each transaction, like "High Risk – 92% chance of fraud."
Business Benefit:	The company can block or flag suspicious transactions instantly, protecting customers' money and reducing losses.
Potential Risk:	The model could be wrong — blocking real purchases (annoying customers) or missing actual fraud (losing money anyway).
